N:HOW READ (2026-11-11): No source plate exists because this is a new composition. Bar-by-bar audit: verse 1: bar 1 C-D-E-G in quarters (the stepwise climb); bar 2 A-A quarters and high C half note; bar 3 C-A-G-E quarters (the climb turned back); bar 4 G-E quarters and D half note, an open ending on re. Chorus: bar 5 G-E quarters and C half note (crescendo begins); bar 6 C-E quarters and G half note (crescendo ends); bar 7 A-A-G-E quarters (diminuendo begins); bar 8 D-E quarters and C half note, the cadence on do. Verse 2: bar 9 repeats bar 1; bar 10 A-high C-A (quarter, quarter, half) varies bar 2; bar 11 high C-A-G and a quarter rest, the "room"; bar 12 G-E-D (quarter, quarter, half), the open ending again. Bars 13-16 repeat the chorus exactly, hairpins included. Every bar totals four quarter beats; the only pitches are C, D, E, G, A and high C; range C4-C5; the largest leap is the perfect fourth D4-G4 into each chorus; durations are quarters, halves and one quarter rest. Originality: a stepwise climb through the pentatonic scale is common property; the tune was checked against "Jarabi" on Kaira, "Hot Cross Buns", "Mary Had a Little Lamb", "Do-Re-Mi" and "Kumbaya" and shares no phrase with them (the chorus cadence is re-mi-do, not mi-re-do).
N:UNCERTAIN: Whether Grade 2 singers keep sixteen bars and two verses in mind, whether the crescendo and diminuendo are audible over a classroom bordun, and whether a seven-year-old soloist can fill the bar 11 rest in time are untested. A Mande music educator, a classroom trial and a second score reader remain required.
